<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words 15th century; first printed <a href="/years/1599">1599</a>; Praetorius's harmony <a href="/years/1609">1609</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Es ist ein Ros entsprungen&quot; (&quot;A rose has sprung up&quot;) is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> and Marian hymn of German origin, best known in English as &quot;Lo, How a Rose E'er Blooming&quot; and also called &quot;A Spotless Rose&quot; and &quot;Behold a Rose of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>&quot;.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The prophecy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> that a rod shall come forth out of the stem of Jesse had been sung in Christian hymns since at least the 8th century, when Cosmas the Melodist wrote of the Virgin Mary flowering from the Root of Jesse. The German text dates from the 15th century and its author is unknown. Its earliest source is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> from the Carthusian monastery of St Alban in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, now kept in the Trier City Library and thought to have been in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s time. It first appeared in print, with the tune now sung to it, in the Speyer Hymnal printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> in 1599.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The hymn originally had two verses. The first tells of a rose sprouting from the stem of the Tree of Jesse, the image of the descent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> from Jesse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, father of King David, which was common in medieval religious art. The second, in the first person, explains that the rose is Mary, the pure maiden, who brings forth the Christ child as a small flower. Catholics have sung it with the focus on Mary, Protestants on Jesus, and Theodore Baker's English translation of 1894 makes the rose the infant Christ. In 1844 the hymnologist Friedrich Layriz added three stanzas, of which Das Blümelein so kleine passed into hymnals of both churches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Michael Praetorius wrote the familiar harmonisation in 1609. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brahms">Johannes Brahms</a> based a chorale prelude for organ on the tune in 1896, one of his Eleven Chorale Preludes, Op. 122, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a>, Hugo Distler and Jan Sandström also used it. In Nazi Germany the carol was rewritten as Uns ist ein Licht erstanden, with a secular text of sunlight on the Fatherland and the virtues of motherhood, as part of the rewriting of carols to remove the Jewish origins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Catherine Winkworth's English version, A Spotless Rose, was set as a motet by Herbert Howells in 1919, and Harriet Reynolds Krauth Spaeth translated it as Behold, a rose is growing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Es ist ein Ros entsprungen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</b>&quot; (&quot;A rose has sprung up&quot;) is a Christmas carol and Marian hymn of German origin, best known in English as &quot;Lo, How a Rose E'er Blooming&quot; and also called &quot;A Spotless Rose&quot; and &quot;Behold a Rose of Judah&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/es-ist-ein-ros-entsprungen">Es ist ein Ros entsprungen</a></li></ul></section>
